  {
   "id": "e2-31",
   "name": "Modals and polite expressions"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Modal verbs go before a base verb and add a meaning:\n• can: ability or permission — “I can swim.” “Can I go out?”\n• could: a more polite request, or past ability — “Could you help me, please?” “I could read at five.”\n• may: polite permission or possibility — “May I come in?” “It may rain.”\n• must: strong obligation — “You must wear a uniform.” mustn't: it is forbidden.\n• should: advice — “You should rest.”\n• would: polite offers and requests — “Would you like some tea?” “Would you open the window, please?”\nAfter a modal, use the base verb with no to and no -s: “She can swim” (not “she cans” or “can to swim”).\nPolite expressions: please, thank you, excuse me, I'm sorry, “Would you mind …?”",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "n": "the principal"
  },
  "problem": "Make three polite requests to {n}: to come in, to borrow a book, and to leave early.",
  "steps": [
   "Permission to enter: “May I come in, please?”",
   "Borrowing: “Could I borrow this book, please?”",
   "Leaving early, with a reason: “Would it be possible for me to leave early? My mother is ill.”"
  ],
  "answer": "May I come in, please? / Could I borrow this book, please? / Would it be possible for me to leave early?",
  "checks": []
 },
 "teach": {
  "cards": [
   {
    "id": "c31-1",
    "status": "draft",
    "idea": "After a modal (can, could, may, must, should, would), use the base verb: no to, no -s.",
    "vars": {},
    "example": [
     "She can sing. (not: cans sing, can to sing)",
     "You should rest."
    ],
